{"id":"refine_20260929114427778","summary":"Added a global memory for recognizing and safely solving ARC template-stamping tasks.","rationale":"The successful solution replaced each isolated marker with a translated copy of the small multicolor template, keeping the original template and clipping copies at grid boundaries. Repeated invalid submissions also show the value of programmatically validating dimensions before submission.","expectedOutcome":"On similar recurring tasks, infer the template anchor and marker correspondence from demos, generate the stamped grid while preserving the source object, and validate its dimensions; check by comparing the constructed output against demo transformations and confirming uniform row widths.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_template_stamping_infer_anchor_copy_and_validate","title":"ARC template stamping: infer anchor, copy, and validate","content":"For recurring ARC tasks with a small multicolor object and isolated marker cells, use demos to infer the object's bounding box and which point in it aligns with each marker. Replace each marker with a translated copy of the object, preserve the original object unless demos indicate otherwise, and clip copies at grid boundaries. Determine overlap behavior from examples rather than assuming it. Generate the output programmatically when possible and validate that it has the intended dimensions and every row has equal width before submission.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This generalizes the demonstrated successful template-copy transformation and addresses dimension-validation failures observed during the task.","after":{"id":"arc_template_stamping_infer_anchor_copy_and_validate","kind":"memory","title":"ARC template stamping: infer anchor, copy, and validate","content":"For recurring ARC tasks with a small multicolor object and isolated marker cells, use demos to infer the object's bounding box and which point in it aligns with each marker. Replace each marker with a translated copy of the object, preserve the original object unless demos indicate otherwise, and clip copies at grid boundaries. Determine overlap behavior from examples rather than assuming it. Generate the output programmatically when possible and validate that it has the intended dimensions and every row has equal width before submission.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:44:34.002Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:44:34.002Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115439688","summary":"Updated the global ARC memory to record that enclosed background regions are filled with the demonstrated fill color, not the outline color.","rationale":"In task-a45610b3, a request for a demonstration established color 4 as the fill color; the first attempt incorrectly used outline color 7, while filling only the rectangle’s interior with 4 solved the task.","expectedOutcome":"For similar ARC tasks, infer the fill color from demonstrations, identify genuinely enclosed background cells, and preserve the outline and unbounded background.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_fill_fully_enclosed_outline_regions","title":"ARC: fill fully enclosed outline regions with the demonstrated fill color","content":"For ARC grids with background cells bounded by an outline or line network, identify genuinely enclosed regions and fill their interior with the color demonstrated by input-output examples. Do not assume the fill color is the outline color or the original background. In task-a45610b3, a demo established color 4 as the fill color: the test contained a color-7 rectangular boundary around color-5 cells, and changing only those interior cells to 4 solved it. Preserve the boundary and all unbounded background; use demonstrations to determine which enclosed regions qualify and the fill color.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated on task-a45610b3 demo and solved test"},"reason":"The trajectory shows a wrong first attempt from using the outline color, followed by a demonstration-guided fill-color correction that solved the puzzle.","before":{"id":"arc_fill_fully_enclosed_outline_regions","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: fill fully enclosed outline regions","content":"For ARC grids with a background, uniform-color line drawing, and demonstrated fill color, identify every background region completely enclosed by the line network and fill its interior, preserving outline and exterior. Use flood fill from the grid border to distinguish exterior background from enclosed holes; fill remaining background components. Do not fill open U-shaped or partially bounded areas. A demo showed outline 3/background 0 with fill 4; test changed outline/background to 5/1 but retained fill 4. The test contained two enclosed holes, both filled.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:49:30.006300+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:49:30.006322+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_fill_fully_enclosed_outline_regions","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: fill fully enclosed outline regions with the demonstrated fill color","content":"For ARC grids with background cells bounded by an outline or line network, identify genuinely enclosed regions and fill their interior with the color demonstrated by input-output examples. Do not assume the fill color is the outline color or the original background. In task-a45610b3, a demo established color 4 as the fill color: the test contained a color-7 rectangular boundary around color-5 cells, and changing only those interior cells to 4 solved it. Preserve the boundary and all unbounded background; use demonstrations to determine which enclosed regions qualify and the fill color.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated on task-a45610b3 demo and solved test"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:49:30.006300+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:54:53.601Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115601780","summary":"Strengthen the global ARC stencil-matching memory with a validated rule for simultaneous motif placements.","rationale":"The task demos and correct submission support matching translations of every occupied motif cell against background cells, then recoloring the union of matches; matching must use the original grid so overlapping placements are handled consistently.","expectedOutcome":"On similar noisy binary-background ARC tasks, identify all valid stencil translations without relying on run lengths, preserve the source motif and unrelated cells, and validate overlap handling against demonstrations.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_colored_shape_stencil_matching_zero_patterns","title":"ARC colored-shape stencil matching zero patterns","content":"For ARC grids with a noisy two-color background and a small connected non-background colored motif, treat the motif’s occupied cells as a stencil, not as a run-length threshold. Enumerate translations for which every occupied stencil coordinate overlaps a background cell (often 0 in demonstrations). Compute all matches against the original grid before painting; recolor the union of matched stencil cells with the motif color so overlaps do not suppress later matches. Preserve the source motif and all other cells. Infer the background and motif colors from the task examples, and verify the placement rule against the demonstrations before applying it broadly. This rule solved task-6508a967, whose motif was a 3-cell L and whose demos used other small motifs.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds the demonstrated all-cells-match and simultaneous-union details, including the important original-grid overlap safeguard.","before":{"id":"arc_colored_shape_stencil_matching_zero_patterns","kind":"memory","title":"ARC colored-shape stencil matching zero patterns","content":"For ARC grids with a noisy two-color background and a small connected non-background colored motif, treat the motif pixels as a stencil, not as a run-length threshold. Find translations of the motif bounding-box pattern whose stencil positions are all the background target color (often 0); recolor those matched cells to the motif color, preserving all other cells. Overlapping matches contribute their union. Keep the motif orientation as shown unless demonstrations prove rotations/reflections. This was validated on task-6508a967: the 1 motif at top-left was a 3x3 stencil and the correct output colored target-zero cells only at exact matching translations. Simple horizontal/vertical run heuristics produced incorrect outputs. Compare several demo pairs and test exact-shape placement before broad stamping.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:48:58.728916+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:48:58.728969+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_colored_shape_stencil_matching_zero_patterns","kind":"memory","title":"ARC colored-shape stencil matching zero patterns","content":"For ARC grids with a noisy two-color background and a small connected non-background colored motif, treat the motif’s occupied cells as a stencil, not as a run-length threshold. Enumerate translations for which every occupied stencil coordinate overlaps a background cell (often 0 in demonstrations). Compute all matches against the original grid before painting; recolor the union of matched stencil cells with the motif color so overlaps do not suppress later matches. Preserve the source motif and all other cells. Infer the background and motif colors from the task examples, and verify the placement rule against the demonstrations before applying it broadly. This rule solved task-6508a967, whose motif was a 3-cell L and whose demos used other small motifs.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:48:58.728916+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:56:07.094Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120045301","summary":"Added a global memory for ARC tasks that transform grids through demo-specific, cellwise palette remapping.","rationale":"The task was solved by applying the mapping demonstrated for the test palette pair; an unsupported initial mapping was incorrect. The examples establish exact mappings for two palette pairs but do not justify a broader rule.","expectedOutcome":"For similar tasks, use demos to identify the exact input-to-output color mapping, apply it consistently while preserving grid dimensions, and request a demo when the applicable mapping is unclear.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_demo_specific_cellwise_palette_remapping","title":"ARC demo-specific cellwise palette remapping","content":"For ARC tasks where demos show a cellwise palette transformation, record the exact mapping for each demonstrated input palette pair; do not infer mappings from color frequency, dominance, or another pair. Observed examples: input colors {5,7} mapped 5→7 and 7→0; input colors {9,5} mapped 9→0 and 5→9. The {9,5} mapping correctly solved a test; an unsupported mapping of 9→7 and 5→0 was incorrect. Apply the demonstrated mapping to every cell, preserve dimensions, and verify the result against the demos. If the test palette pair or mapping is not established and remains ambiguous, request a demo.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The trajectory provides successful evidence for exact palette mappings and a failed submission from assuming an unsupported mapping.","after":{"id":"arc_demo_specific_cellwise_palette_remapping","kind":"memory","title":"ARC demo-specific cellwise palette remapping","content":"For ARC tasks where demos show a cellwise palette transformation, record the exact mapping for each demonstrated input palette pair; do not infer mappings from color frequency, dominance, or another pair. Observed examples: input colors {5,7} mapped 5→7 and 7→0; input colors {9,5} mapped 9→0 and 5→9. The {9,5} mapping correctly solved a test; an unsupported mapping of 9→7 and 5→0 was incorrect. Apply the demonstrated mapping to every cell, preserve dimensions, and verify the result against the demos. If the test palette pair or mapping is not established and remains ambiguous, request a demo.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:00:50.712Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:00:50.712Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929121339483","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for 3H×2W outputs formed from three vertical bands and two horizontal blocks.","rationale":"The demonstrations and solved test support a repeatable tiling rule: the outer vertical bands use input rows in reverse order, the middle band uses them in order, and each band pairs a horizontally reversed row on the left with the unchanged row on the right. Earlier attempts failed by assuming the same vertical arrangement across all bands, so the memory stresses checking each band against demos.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC tasks, infer and verify the ordering of each vertical band and the left/right row transforms before constructing a 3H×2W grid; validate dimensions and demo rows before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_three_by_two_row_reversal_tiling","title":"ARC 3H×2W tiling with reversed outer bands and mirrored left blocks","content":"For ARC tasks whose demos expand an H×W input to 3H×2W, test whether each output row is made from two copies of an input row: a horizontally reversed copy on the left and the unchanged row on the right. The demonstrated vertical pattern was [input rows in reverse order, input rows in normal order, input rows in reverse order]. Thus output band b uses input row H−1−i for the outer bands and row i for the middle band, with output row [reverse(source_row) + source_row]. Do not assume all three bands have the same vertical ordering or infer the rule from dimensions alone: map several demo rows to input rows and verify all bands and both horizontal halves before applying it to a test.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This rule solved the test correctly after repeated failed guesses; demos establish both the differing outer/middle vertical order and the horizontal mirror-plus-copy construction.","after":{"id":"arc_three_by_two_row_reversal_tiling","kind":"memory","title":"ARC 3H×2W tiling with reversed outer bands and mirrored left blocks","content":"For ARC tasks whose demos expand an H×W input to 3H×2W, test whether each output row is made from two copies of an input row: a horizontally reversed copy on the left and the unchanged row on the right. The demonstrated vertical pattern was [input rows in reverse order, input rows in normal order, input rows in reverse order]. Thus output band b uses input row H−1−i for the outer bands and row i for the middle band, with output row [reverse(source_row) + source_row]. Do not assume all three bands have the same vertical ordering or infer the rule from dimensions alone: map several demo rows to input rows and verify all bands and both horizontal halves before applying it to a test.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:13:47.204Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:13:47.204Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929122334567","summary":"Replace the unresolved task-9c84caa6 memory with the demonstrated selective shape-recoloring rule and safeguards.","rationale":"Across the demos, a component matching the marker/divider shape is recolored to the marker color while unrelated components are preserved. In this test, the color-8 L matches the color-7 reference and changes; the congruent color-3 L does not. Earlier attempts failed by recoloring the wrong component or too many cells, so selection must be inferred from demonstrations rather than shape similarity alone.","expectedOutcome":"For recurring instances of this task family, identify the reference shape and marker color, infer which matching occurrence is selected from demos, recolor only that component, and validate that all other cells and grid dimensions remain unchanged.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_9c84caa6_sparse_multicolor_component_recoloring_observed_rule_unresolve","title":"ARC task-9c84caa6: selectively recolor a marker-shape counterpart","content":"For recurring task-9c84caa6 grids, demonstrations support selectively recoloring a component whose shape matches a conspicuous marker or divider structure, using the marker's color, while preserving unrelated cells and components. Infer from demos which matching occurrence is selected; do not recolor every congruent object or assume that a visually similar object is the target. In the solved test, the color-7 divider/L structure indicated recoloring the upper-left color-8 L triomino to 7, while the congruent color-3 L remained unchanged. Earlier guesses that recolored the color-3 object, changed the divider, or recolored the template itself were wrong. Preserve grid dimensions and validate that only the demonstrated target component changes.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Resolved the previously unresolved recurring task memory using multiple demonstrations and the confirmed final answer, while recording failure modes that caused wrong submissions.","before":{"id":"arc_task_9c84caa6_sparse_multicolor_component_recoloring_observed_rule_unresolve","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-9c84caa6: sparse multicolor component recoloring observed, rule unresolved","content":"Task id task-9c84caa6 recurs with fresh grids. Demos are large grids filled with a background color and scattered color components. Outputs preserve dimensions and nearly all pixels, changing only selected component(s) or individual cells; changes are not a simple global palette map. Evidence from the 8 demos: demo1 a small 4 component became 9; demo2 a 9 component became 0; demo3 a lone 8 pixel became 9; demo4 a 6 pair disappeared (became background) and a 4 vertical-three component became 1; demo5 a 9 horizontal-three component became 8; demo6 a large 9 component became 0; demo7 one 0 pixel became 2; demo8 a 5 block component became 8. These do not establish a reliable rule. On test 19x17, eight guesses (unchanged; remove isolated 0; recolor lone 2, large 8 component, etc.) were wrong. Future recurrence: request demos early, compare exact changed coordinates/components and search for geometric/overlap correspondence across all colors; do not guess global recolors or alter arbitrary singletons. All previous conjectures about matching shapes were unsupported.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:18:36.801306+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:18:36.801322+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_task_9c84caa6_sparse_multicolor_component_recoloring_observed_rule_unresolve","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-9c84caa6: selectively recolor a marker-shape counterpart","content":"For recurring task-9c84caa6 grids, demonstrations support selectively recoloring a component whose shape matches a conspicuous marker or divider structure, using the marker's color, while preserving unrelated cells and components. Infer from demos which matching occurrence is selected; do not recolor every congruent object or assume that a visually similar object is the target. In the solved test, the color-7 divider/L structure indicated recoloring the upper-left color-8 L triomino to 7, while the congruent color-3 L remained unchanged. Earlier guesses that recolored the color-3 object, changed the divider, or recolored the template itself were wrong. Preserve grid dimensions and validate that only the demonstrated target component changes.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:18:36.801306+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:23:43.203Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929125509034","summary":"Added a global memory for task-44d81bbe motif replication.","rationale":"The solved test and three demonstrations show isolated markers being replaced by translated copies of a source motif; the examples support center alignment for interior markers and show that placement near boundaries must be checked against demonstrations.","expectedOutcome":"On recurring instances, identify the source motif and marker cells, infer orientation and alignment from the demonstrations, preserve the source, and validate boundary placements before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_44d81bbe_replicate_source_motif_at_markers","title":"ARC task-44d81bbe: replicate a source motif at isolated markers","content":"For task-44d81bbe, identify the uniform background, the source motif, and isolated marker cells. Replace each marker with a translated copy of the source motif while preserving the source and unrelated background cells. Infer the motif's orientation, exact colors, and marker-to-motif alignment from demonstrations rather than assuming a universal anchor: the demonstrated straight five-cell motifs align their center with interior markers, while boundary placements may require shifting or clipping and should be verified against the examples. Validate the full output, especially marker replacement, dimensions, and edge cases. Demonstrated motifs include [1,4,6,6,9] and [8,6,8,6,8]; the solved test used vertical motif [7,1,7,7,7].","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The trajectory solved this recurring pattern after demonstrations clarified motif replication and center alignment; preserving the boundary-placement caveat avoids overgeneralizing from interior markers.","after":{"id":"arc_task_44d81bbe_replicate_source_motif_at_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-44d81bbe: replicate a source motif at isolated markers","content":"For task-44d81bbe, identify the uniform background, the source motif, and isolated marker cells. Replace each marker with a translated copy of the source motif while preserving the source and unrelated background cells. Infer the motif's orientation, exact colors, and marker-to-motif alignment from demonstrations rather than assuming a universal anchor: the demonstrated straight five-cell motifs align their center with interior markers, while boundary placements may require shifting or clipping and should be verified against the examples. Validate the full output, especially marker replacement, dimensions, and edge cases. Demonstrated motifs include [1,4,6,6,9] and [8,6,8,6,8]; the solved test used vertical motif [7,1,7,7,7].","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:55:23.076Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:55:23.076Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130037841","summary":"Create a global memory recording task-12adf3d1 as unresolved and documenting the demonstrated recolorings and failed heuristics.","rationale":"Eight demos show dimension-preserving outputs with localized recolorings, but they do not establish a reliable general selection rule; the trajectory’s test submissions were all incorrect.","expectedOutcome":"On recurrence, use the recorded examples as evidence while treating the rule as unresolved, and avoid guessing based only on duplicate shapes or line-like objects.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_12adf3d1_component_recoloring_unresolved","title":"ARC task-12adf3d1: localized component recoloring rule unresolved","content":"Task-12adf3d1 remains unresolved. Across eight demos, grids have a uniform background, many disconnected multicolor motifs, and thin marker lines. Outputs preserve dimensions and almost all cells while locally recoloring an object or component. Observed changes: demo 1, a five-cell color-4 component became 3; demo 2, a singleton 3 became 5; demo 3, a singleton 4 became 6; demo 4, a color-7 component became 6; demo 5, a color-1 shape became 4; demo 6, a singleton 3 became 8; demo 7, a color-5 component became 3; demo 8, a two-cell color-3 component became 6. These examples do not reveal a dependable component-selection or replacement-color rule. Hypotheses based on recoloring identical-shape duplicates or simply changing an apparent repeated motif or line-shaped object were unreliable; all test submissions in the recorded instance were incorrect, so there is no test ground truth. On recurrence, compare demonstrations carefully to infer both which component is selected and its replacement color; do not guess from apparent shape repetition alone.","metadata":{"scope":"global","task_id":"task-12adf3d1","status":"unresolved"},"reason":"Preserves task-specific demonstration evidence and warns against heuristics that led to repeated incorrect submissions, without asserting an unsupported rule.","after":{"id":"arc_task_12adf3d1_component_recoloring_unresolved","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-12adf3d1: localized component recoloring rule unresolved","content":"Task-12adf3d1 remains unresolved. Across eight demos, grids have a uniform background, many disconnected multicolor motifs, and thin marker lines. Outputs preserve dimensions and almost all cells while locally recoloring an object or component. Observed changes: demo 1, a five-cell color-4 component became 3; demo 2, a singleton 3 became 5; demo 3, a singleton 4 became 6; demo 4, a color-7 component became 6; demo 5, a color-1 shape became 4; demo 6, a singleton 3 became 8; demo 7, a color-5 component became 3; demo 8, a two-cell color-3 component became 6. These examples do not reveal a dependable component-selection or replacement-color rule. Hypotheses based on recoloring identical-shape duplicates or simply changing an apparent repeated motif or line-shaped object were unreliable; all test submissions in the recorded instance were incorrect, so there is no test ground truth. On recurrence, compare demonstrations carefully to infer both which component is selected and its replacement color; do not guess from apparent shape repetition alone.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","task_id":"task-12adf3d1","status":"unresolved"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:01:07.544Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:01:07.544Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130753545","summary":"Updated the global 3H×2W ARC tiling memory with the confirmed row ordering and horizontal construction.","rationale":"The task-a8a91d13 demonstration shows an 18×16 output from a 6×8 input: each output row joins a reversed input row on the left with the unchanged row on the right, and the three vertical bands use reversed, forward, then reversed input-row order. Its constant 2×1 test also solved as a 6×2 grid, though that test alone does not distinguish orientations.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC tasks, apply the demonstrated row and band transformations, while checking orientation and band ordering against examples before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_3h_2w_mirrored_row_tiling","title":"ARC 3H×2W mirrored row tiling","content":"For ARC tasks where demonstrations show an H×W grid expanded to 3H×2W, each output row concatenates the selected input row reversed on the left with that row unchanged on the right. The vertical input-row index sequence is H−1..0, then 0..H−1, then H−1..0. Confirmed by task-a8a91d13: a 6×8 demo produced an 18×16 output with this rule; its 2×1 all-2 test produced a 6×2 all-2 grid. Verify horizontal orientation and band ordering from demonstrations before applying, since constant or symmetric inputs may not distinguish alternatives.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Persist the explicitly requested confirmed transformation and retain a caution to validate orientation and band ordering from demos.","before":{"id":"arc_3h_2w_mirrored_row_tiling","kind":"memory","title":"ARC 3H×2W mirrored row tiling","content":"For ARC tasks where demos show an H×W grid expanded to 3H×2W, each output row is formed by concatenating the input row reversed with the input row unchanged. The vertical sequence is input rows reversed, then input rows forward, then reversed again. This matched task-09866993: demo 9×3→27×6 and test 6×15→18×30. Verify row sequence and dimensions against demos before submission.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:14:07.335484+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:14:12.778219+00:00","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_3h_2w_mirrored_row_tiling","kind":"memory","title":"ARC 3H×2W mirrored row tiling","content":"For ARC tasks where demonstrations show an H×W grid expanded to 3H×2W, each output row concatenates the selected input row reversed on the left with that row unchanged on the right. The vertical input-row index sequence is H−1..0, then 0..H−1, then H−1..0. Confirmed by task-a8a91d13: a 6×8 demo produced an 18×16 output with this rule; its 2×1 all-2 test produced a 6×2 all-2 grid. Verify horizontal orientation and band ordering from demonstrations before applying, since constant or symmetric inputs may not distinguish alternatives.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:14:07.335484+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:00.972Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/44ad28d291934fa5ac58a5f0a3f63632/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130901300","summary":"Updated the task-44d81bbe memory with the demonstrated marker-alignment rule and the validated correction.","rationale":"Three demo pairs show that each marker is replaced by a copy of the source motif aligned to its demonstrated internal anchor; for the odd-length line motifs, the geometric center aligns with the marker. The initial test attempt used an incorrect end anchor, while the corrected centered placement solved the task.","expectedOutcome":"On recurrence, infer the source motif and anchor correspondence from demonstrations, stamp a copy at every marker while preserving the source, and clip copies at grid boundaries when necessary.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_44d81bbe_replicate_source_motif_at_markers","title":"ARC task-44d81bbe: replicate source motif at markers using demonstrated anchor","content":"For task-44d81bbe, identify the source motif, isolated marker cells, and their demonstrated anchor correspondence. Replace each marker with a translated copy of the source motif, aligning the motif's anchor—not automatically its distinctive endpoint—to the marker; preserve the source and unrelated background. Demos confirm this across orientations and shapes: the horizontal [2,1,6] motif aligns its middle cell 1 to each marker; an irregular multicolor motif aligns its internal color-1 anchor to isolated marker 0; and a vertical three-cell line aligns its geometric center to each marker. In the test, the vertical [7,7,1] motif was centered with its middle 7 over each marker, and this placement solved the task after an incorrect end-aligned attempt. Infer the anchor from demos, preserve grid dimensions, and clip placements at boundaries if needed.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds validated alignment evidence and records the specific end-anchor failure to help solve recurring instances without repeating it.","before":{"id":"arc_task_44d81bbe_replicate_source_motif_at_ma